coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $S$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {e}_{1} & {e}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{S\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-113513/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f989cbaf-2d99-5267-8864-48a48db2d535"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-113513"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-113515/selection/93e36c11d2f1b4f58abc45c000e9b138e205ae3b488e1e603c4ddda792cc3834" :answer/id #uuid "bc95c339-47e2-5e1c-9edf-6ea25645f0ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113515/selection/c09ad4f053d694f0a3e546376cf7de7d2bf1ddb3950bb399ac56c2972986628a" :answer/id #uuid "b8ccb825-9eef-53f4-88ca-2646956a5d90" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 17\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113515/selection/ab830d8e683194087d27d2eef9a96eae351e832a6a77bd439abfdd759f94697d" :answer/id #uuid "44d21186-f6d2-545e-81fd-6eefd87f7f57" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 17\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113515/selection/33a1ec9da3932b0075e7e89f12d4998987b1bbeb4db77c68f680e4beac95b8c2" :answer/id #uuid "22fc78bb-6bc1-551d-85fa-79b71640f31f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 16\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113515/selection/4586d44427753399e624556630661fac5af751d98d871dfd0c0ffae769f92f09" :answer/id #uuid "37656f9d-4cfa-5ffd-8809-e34784c3f22c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & \\frac{1}{17}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113515/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "574e9abf-ae04-5396-b5b5-53a11c296b27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-113515/selection/c09ad4f053d694f0a3e546376cf7de7d2bf1ddb3950bb399ac56c2972986628a" :answer-field/choices ["q-113515/selection/93e36c11d2f1b4f58abc45c000e9b138e205ae3b488e1e603c4ddda792cc3834" "q-113515/selection/c09ad4f053d694f0a3e546376cf7de7d2bf1ddb3950bb399ac56c2972986628a" "q-113515/selection/ab830d8e683194087d27d2eef9a96eae351e832a6a77bd439abfdd759f94697d" "q-113515/selection/33a1ec9da3932b0075e7e89f12d4998987b1bbeb4db77c68f680e4beac95b8c2" "q-113515/selection/4586d44427753399e624556630661fac5af751d98d871dfd0c0ffae769f92f09"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-113515" :question/id #uuid "c98acfad-2f66-5567-b9e7-daf3db02ed5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-113515" :question/problem "Let $B={[\\begin{aligned}17 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$  be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $S$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{e}_{1},{e}_{2}}$, respectively. Recall that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $S$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {e}_{1} & {e}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}17 & 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{17}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{4}{17} & \\frac{1}{17} & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{4}{17} & \\frac{1}{17} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{17} & -\\frac{4}{17} & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=17{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{4}{17} & \\frac{1}{17} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 17\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{4}{17}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 17\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{S\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 17\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-113515/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f989cbaf-2d99-5267-8864-48a48db2d535"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-113515"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-4832"] :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2\\}$ and $\\mathcal{C}=\\{\\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2\\}$ be bases of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ If $\\mathbf{c}_1=\\mathbf{b}_2-4\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{c}_2=5\\mathbf{b}_1+7\\mathbf{b}_2,$ find the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{C}\\to\\mathcal{B}}$ from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of the vectors in $\\mathcal{C}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ are\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/assets/e62970e0d84f224be492efe709d4df42872cbcc9e68c136e2f73b0e36e18f5c4.png)\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $\\mathcal{C}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/assets/5f5ad323ae8875717d552f068c827295954ecdb2c802f5fb3899c18884aa1f1f.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-4833"] :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2,\\mathbf{b}_3\\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and let $\\mathcal{S}$ be the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ Find the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ if \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/assets/827f1a79a51ea88e59f5d766ee531cacf3945cc2ce649521c7b1d8e7eea35e06.png)\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/assets/82d0eab6a51dbf1615a3e2bb4fc6ffef622a38ac3c980dc742b552946c11a87a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Recall  that the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ consists of the vectors $\\mathbf e_1, \\mathbf e_2,$ and $\\mathbf e_3,$ given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/assets/0d9a23830f48d4659dd963110d9322c12e8d4849bd9bcd63ae9d5adb0e1fba05.png)\n\nThe components of the vectors in $\\mathcal{B}$ are just the coordinates relative to the standard basis:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/assets/10b5f2e35da56c7be6d547827164c68f475df4d012116b9666c12a94bbc39f55.png)\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{S}.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/assets/06ef110e61fcdf044e15300d50bc5ac918b37e8351c1aa6b77124dd2029c9aa3.png)\n\nFinally, $a=0,$ $b=0,$ and $c=10.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-4835"] :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/assets/07a8e394234737d51a0064816b255569d20e826eb3a82884a24f1fc0a9fa6baa.png)\nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}$ is the inverse of the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}.$ Therefore, we simply need to find the inverse of the given matrix.\n\nComputing the inverse of $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-4)\\cdot 5-(-6)\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2} & -3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-4837"] :question/problem "Let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/assets/51b47f81b941fffa62d1f8402a4ef08b9b7d1636b1a37a86578311f1db192b3d.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/assets/a9964c0672c8859f4b4857d422e25a435ed0be907c074a22b7cc12729f051613.png) be bases of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ and $\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\},$ respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$\n\nSo, we need to find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/assets/60eb4bbd057feab1b78fb283e951dc8c35f9a95875bd46f078c87c25b3b749c8.png)\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 7 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 13\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 & 22 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 13\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}= & [\\begin{aligned}\\,4 & 22 \\\\ \\,1 & 13\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
